### A Growing Concern: Food Recalls in 2026

The year is still ongoing, and we've already seen a staggering **577 food recalls**. To put that in perspective, we've seen about 60% of that number as Class II recalls, with 361 instances reported. Class I, the most severe, has reached **176 cases** so far, portending a troubling trend in food safety. For comparison, the historic total of food recalls stands at **935**, so we're burning through those numbers at an alarming rate.

### Recidivism in the Food Industry

Of the recalls this year, **87 are attributed to repeat offenders**. These are firms that have faced multiple recalls over the years, indicating a worrying trend of recurring issues. Among the worst offenders are Moonlight Packing Corporation (11 recalls, all Class I), Albertsons Companies LLC (11 recalls, with 10 Class I), and Ghirardelli Chocolate Company (10 recalls, 10 Class I). It begs the question: what happens to encourage these brands to change?

### June's Recall Roundup

Recent activity includes **Galaxy RG Corp**, which issued a slew of recalls on June 17, 2026, for undeclared synthetic food colors (Yellow #5, Yellow #6, and Red #40) across a range of desserts. This case underscores the ongoing challenge of undeclared allergens and additives in products. Meanwhile, **Total Nutrition Inc** had a more serious recall for potential **Salmonella contamination** in their moringa capsules, a Class I recall indicating life-threatening risks. 

And who could ignore the addition of **SENSORY SUPPLEMENT LLC** to the mix, recalling their multi-use dietary supplement due to "premature lipid oxidation," which could cause an off odor and bitter taste? While it's a Class III recall (currently unclassified), it does raise alarm bells about quality control.

### A Look Ahead

With **only halfway through the year**, the possibility of hitting 1,000 total recalls in the next few years seems less of a possibility and more of a grim reality. With the food industry continuing to churn recalls at such alarming rates, one wonders: when will standards catch up to the scale of the problem?
